[Line "9.Ne1 Nd7 10.Be3: 13.Rc1 and the c5 pawn sacrifice"]
[Section "Mar del Plata: 9.Ne1"]
[Summary "The modern main line. White gives the c-pawn to open the queenside at once; Black takes it, parks a knight on a6 and attacks with ...h5 and ...g4."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 { Black can play this without preparation:
7.dxe5 dxe5 8.Qxd8 Rxd8 9.Nxe5 does not win a pawn because of 9...Nxe4 (see the Exchange Variation). }
7. O-O { White keeps the tension, so Black increases it. } 7... Nc6 { [%cal Rc6d4,Re5d4] The repertoire move.
A second attack on d4 means White cannot go on making useful moves: he has to close the centre (8.d5),
release it (8.dxe5) or defend it with a piece that can be harassed (8.Be3 Ng4). Closing it gains a tempo on
the knight, but a closed centre is exactly what Black wants, because the f6-knight can then leave without
e5 or e4 becoming a problem and the f-pawn can advance. }
( 7... Na6 { A sound second system, not the repertoire. The knight waits for d5 to go to c5 and Black keeps
...Qe8, ...Ng4 and ...c6 in reserve. It avoids the long forcing lines, at the price of leaving White the
choice of structure. } )
( 7... Nbd7 { The old main line: solid, with ...c6, ...Re8 and ...exd4 to come. The knight blocks the
c8-bishop and puts no pressure on d4, so White builds up at leisure with Re1, Bf1 and Rb1. } )
( 7... exd4 { Gives up the centre for play against e4. } 8. Nxd4 Re8 9. f3 { White has more space and a
small, lasting pull. Black's set-up with ...c6 and ...d5 or ...Nbd7-c5 is playable but it is a different
opening from the one in this chapter. } )
8. d5 Ne7 { [%cal Gf7f5,Rc4c5] [%csl Yd6,Yc7] The Mar del Plata. On e7 the knight looks clumsy, but it is
the right square: it supports ...f5 and goes to g6 as soon as the f-pawn has passed, whereas from b8 it would
need three moves to do anything.

**Plans:** White attacks the base of Black's chain with c5, opens the c-file and aims at c7, d6 and a7.
Black attacks the base of White's with ...f5-f4 and ...g5-g4. White is usually faster; Black is playing for
mate. }
9. Ne1 { [%cal Re1d3,Rf2f3] The knight goes to d3 to support c5 and f4, and the f-pawn is free to defend
e4. This is the line where the race is run in its purest form. } 9... Nd7 { [%cal Gf7f5,Gd7f6] The knight
has to release the f-pawn, and from d7 it also covers c5, so that White needs b4 or Nd3 before he can
break. It will return to f6 once ...f4 has been played. }
( 9... Ne8 { The alternative, and Kasparov played both. From e8 the knight guards c7 and d6 in advance,
but it does nothing to hold up c5, so White gets his break a move sooner. Worth knowing as a surprise;
the plans are the same. } )
10. Be3 { [%cal Re3a7] The most ambitious square. The bishop supports c5, eyes a7 and will defend the king
from f2. The drawback is that ...f4 comes with tempo. } 10... f5 11. f3 { White must keep e4 protected by a
pawn. 11.exf5 gxf5 only helps Black, who gets e4 covered and a mobile centre with ...f4 or ...Nf6 and ...e4
in the air. } 11... f4 { Never ...fxe4: after fxe4 White has the f-file and Black has no pawn break left. }
12. Bf2 g5 { [%cal Gg5g4,Gh7h5] [%csl Rg2] The attack is under way. Black intends ...Ng6, ...Nf6, ...Rf7,
...Bf8, ...Rg7 and ...h5 in whatever order White's threats allow, and then ...g4. }
13. Rc1 { [%cal Rc4c5] The modern main line. White wants c5 without spending a move on b4 or Nd3, and with
the rook already on the c-file the pawn can be offered. } 13... Ng6 { [%cal Gg6h4] The move order matters.
After 13...Nf6 14.c5 Ng6 15.cxd6 cxd6 16.Nb5 White has the c-file and the b5-knight for free and Black is
a tempo short. By keeping the knight on d7 for one more move Black makes White pay for c5 with a pawn. }
( 13... Rf6 { A different way to attack, with pieces: ...Rh6 or ...Rg6 and ...Qe8-h5, while the knight
stays on d7 to hold c5. It is rarer and less well mapped; a reasonable second weapon. } )
14. c5 { White gives the pawn to open lines before Black has finished regrouping. }
( 14. Nd3 { The slow way. } 14... Nf6 15. c5 { The 13.Nd3 line by transposition, see there. } 15... Rf7 )
( 14. Nb5 { Aiming at a7 as well as c7. } 14... b6 { Not 14...a6, when the knight goes to a7 and takes the
c8-bishop; the trick is shown under 13.Nb5. After ...b6 the knight will be driven back by ...a6 when it
suits Black. } )
14... Nxc5 { With the knight, so that the chain d6-c7 stays intact. } 15. b4 Na6 { [%cal Ga6b4] [%csl Yc7]
The only square that works: 15...Nd7 16.Nb5 wins back c7 or a7 at once with a bind. From a6 the knight
guards c7 and attacks b4. It is out of play for the attack, but Black is a pawn up and still has
everything he needs on the kingside. }
16. Nb5 { [%cal Rb5a7,Rb5c7] The usual move: the knight covers b4's weakness by threatening a7. }
( 16. Nd3 { Protects b4 first and keeps Nb5 for later. } 16... Rf7 { Covering c7 before it is attacked.
Black continues ...h5, ...Bf8 or ...Bh6 and ...g4, and meets Nb5 with ...Bd7. } )
16... Bd7 { [%cal Gd7b5] Developing with a threat to take the knight is one of the few times the bishop
leaves c8 willingly, and it can go back. 17.Nxa7 regains the pawn but sends the knight to the edge of the
board, three moves from the kingside. } 17. a4 { [%cal Gh7h5,Gg5g4,Gf8f7] White supports the knight and
keeps the bind.

**Verdict:** White has lasting pressure for the pawn: the a6-knight is tied down and b5 is a fine outpost.
Black has an extra pawn and an untouched attack: ...h5 and ...Rf7 come next, then ...g4, and the bishop
can take on b5 whenever the knight becomes dangerous. All the recent repertoire books
treat 13.Rc1 as the critical test of 10.Be3; in practice both sides win games from this position. } *

[Line "10.Be3: 13.a4 a5"]
[Section "Mar del Plata: 9.Ne1"]
[Summary "Korchnoi's 13.a4 prepares a5 and c5. Black stops the pawn with ...a5 and ...b6, accepts a knight on c6 and gets on with the attack."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Be3 f5 11. f3
f4 12. Bf2 g5 13. a4 { [%cal Ra4a5,Rc4c5] Korchnoi's move. The a-pawn is to go to a5, after which c5
arrives with more force, a6 can break up the queenside and the bishop on f2 bears on b6 and a7. }
13... a5 { [%csl Yb6,Yc5] The modern answer. The queenside is now much harder to open: White needs b4, and
after ...axb4 Black has the a-file and the c5-square is still covered. The price is the hole on b5. }
( 13... Ng6 { The older move, which allows 14.a5. It is playable, and it was the scene of a model attack. }
14. Nd3 Nf6 15. c5 h5 16. h3 Rf7 17. c6 a5 { Keeping the queenside closed. } 18. cxb7 Bxb7 19. b4 Bc8
{ The bishop goes straight back to the diagonal where it belongs. } 20. bxa5 Bh6 21. Nb4 g4 22. Nc6 Qf8
23. fxg4 hxg4 24. hxg4 Bg5 25. Bf3 Qh6 { Korchnoi-Kasparov, Amsterdam 1991. The bishop came to g5 via h6
and the queen to h6 via f8; White's king did not survive the h-file. } )
14. Nd3 { Heading for b4 after the pawn exchange, or supporting c5. } 14... b6 { [%cal Ga5b4] Now c5 is
ruled out for a long time and b4 can be met by ...axb4. The move also takes b6 away from White's bishop. }
15. b4
( 15. Be1 { White wants to recapture on b4 with the bishop and free f2 for the knight. } 15... h5 { Black
simply continues; ...Nf6, ...Ng6 and ...g4 follow. } )
15... axb4 16. Nxb4 Nf6 { The knight has done its work on d7 and returns to support ...g4. } 17. Nc6
{ [%csl Rc6] The point of White's play: the knight cannot be left there. } 17... Nxc6 18. dxc6 { [%cal Gd8e8,Gc8e6,Gg5g4]
**Verdict:** the structure has changed in Black's favour in one respect and White's in another. The pawn
on c6 cramps the queenside and gives White d5 for the other knight, but it is cut off from its
supporters, Black's d6-pawn is no longer a target and the centre is closed for good. Black attacks the
pawn with ...Qe8 or covers d5 with ...Be6, and then plays ...h5 and ...g4. Chances are level. } *

[Line "10.Be3: 13.Nd3 and 13.b4, the classical race"]
[Section "Mar del Plata: 9.Ne1"]
[Summary "White prepares c5 the slow way and Black has time for the full Gligoric set-up. Two famous games show how the attack is carried out."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Be3 f5 11. f3
f4 12. Bf2 g5 13. Nd3 { The old main line. The knight supports c5, but it has taken a move and it no
longer defends g2 from e1. }
( 13. b4 { The other way to prepare c5. } 13... Nf6 14. c5 Ng6 15. cxd6 cxd6 16. Rc1 Rf7 17. a4 Bf8 18. a5
Bd7 { Here the bishop can come out, because it gains time against Nb5. } 19. Nb5 g4 { Black does not
defend c7 a second time. } 20. Nc7 g3 { [%csl Rh2,Rf2] The rook is left to its fate. The pawn on g3 opens
the diagonal for the queen and the knight comes to h5. } 21. Nxa8 Nh5 22. Kh1 gxf2 23. Rxf2 Ng3+ 24. Kg1
Qxa8 { Piket-Kasparov, Tilburg 1989. Black has two pieces for the rook and the dark squares; he won in a
few more moves. White's play can be improved, but the game shows how little the queenside matters once
...g3 has been played. } )
13... Nf6 { Now that c5 can no longer be prevented, the knight goes where it is needed for ...g4. } 14. c5
Ng6 15. Rc1 Rf7 { [%cal Gg7f8,Gf7g7] [%csl Yc7] Gligoric's move, and the key to the whole system. The rook
covers c7 along the rank, so the open c-file gives White no entry, and it clears f8 for the bishop, which
in turn will clear g7 for the rook. } 16. Rc2 { White doubles on the c-file. 16.Kh1 is the modern
refinement, making room for the bishop on g1; Black's set-up does not change. } 16... Bf8 { Covers d6
and so frees the knight on f6 and the queen from defensive duty. } 17. cxd6 cxd6 { Always with the pawn:
the chain stays whole. } 18. Qd2 g4 19. Rfc1 g3 { [%cal Gf6h5,Gd8h4,Gc8h3] The standard pawn sacrifice.
Black does not want the g-file opened by exchanges, he wants the dark squares and the h-file. } 20. hxg3
fxg3 21. Bxg3 Nh5 22. Bh2 Be7 { [%cal Ge7g5,Gg5e3] Taimanov-Najdorf, Zurich 1953. The bishop comes to g5
and e3, the queen to g5 or h4, the knights to f4 and h4, and the c8-bishop waits for h3.

**Verdict:** White's doubled rooks hit nothing, because c7 is covered and there is no second target, while
every black piece has a route to the king. This is why White looked for faster methods (13.Rc1, 13.a4)
and why the line is still worth playing against anyone who chooses it. } *

[Line "10.Be3: 13.g4 and 13.Nb5"]
[Section "Mar del Plata: 9.Ne1"]
[Summary "White blocks the kingside before attacking on the other wing, or probes a7 first. Black switches to the h-file, and answers Nb5 with ...b6."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Be3 f5 11. f3
f4 12. Bf2 g5 13. g4 { [%csl Yg4] White stops ...g4 once and for all and means to play on the queenside
with his king safe. The cost is that his own king's position has become rigid. }
( 13. Nb5 { [%cal Rb5a7] The knight attacks a7, which the bishop on f2 is also eyeing. } 13... b6 { The
right pawn. The knight will be driven away with ...a6 next, and Black has gained two moves that are
useful against c5. White argues that b6 and a6 are targets for a4-a5; theory considers the position
balanced. }
( 13... a6? { The natural move loses the piece Black cannot do without. } 14. Na7 { The knight is immune:
14...Rxa7 15.Bxa7 b6 traps the bishop only for a moment, as b4 and c5 will dig it out and White has won
the exchange. Otherwise Nxc8 follows and the attack has lost its bishop. } )
14. b4 a6 15. Nc3 { Black continues with ...Ng6, ...Nf6 and ...h5 as usual. } )
13... h5 { [%cal Gf8f6,Gf6h6] Keeping the pawns on the board and putting the question to g4 at once. }
( 13... fxg3 { The other treatment, taken en passant: after 14.hxg3 Black plays ...Ng6 and ...h5-h4 or
...Nf6-h5. It opens the position for White's bishops as well, and it gives up the f4-pawn that cramps
him, so it is the second choice. } 14. hxg3 Ng6 )
14. h3 Rf6 { [%cal Gf6h6,Ge7g6,Gg7f8] The plan is the h-file: ...Rh6, ...Ng6, ...Nf6 and, when everything is
ready, ...hxg4 followed by an invasion on h2 or h4, or a sacrifice on g4.

**Verdict:** the attack is slower than usual and White's queenside play is as fast as ever, so Black
must not hurry: he first brings every piece to the kingside and keeps c7 and d6 covered with the knight
on d7 and the bishop on f8. The position is closed and manoeuvring, with chances for both sides. } *

[Line "10.Nd3 f5 11.Bd2: the main line"]
[Section "Mar del Plata: 9.Ne1"]
[Summary "The bishop stays out of range of ...f4 and White breaks with c5 at once. Black regroups with ...Rf7, ...Ne8, ...Bf8 and ...Rg7 and sacrifices a pawn with ...g4."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 { The
knight supports c5 immediately and the bishop will go to d2, where ...f4 does not gain a tempo. } 10... f5
11. Bd2 { 11.exf5 is met by 11...Nxf5 with ...Nd4 to come, and after 11.f4 exf4 Black gets the e5-square for a
knight: opening the centre does not suit White here. } 11... Nf6 { [%cal Rf6e4] Back at once: the
knight attacks e4, and c5 cannot be held up anyway now that the knight is on d3. } 12. f3 f4 13. c5
( 13. g4 { The blockade. } 13... g5 { The main line. The pawn stays on f4, where it cramps White, and Black will
use the h-file. } 14. Be1 h5 15. h3 Ng6 16. Kg2 Rf7 17. c5 Bf8 18. Bf2 Rh7 { [%cal Gh7h8,Gd8e8] Black doubles on
the h-file and decides later whether to open it. The king's side is no longer a place where White can be
mated quickly, but neither can he ignore it. } )
13... g5 14. Rc1
( 14. cxd6 cxd6 15. Nf2 { White defends first: the knight covers g4 and h3. } 15... h5 16. h3 Ng6 17. Qc2
Rf7 { Against Nb5 and Qc7. Black continues ...a6, ...Bf8 and ...g4 as a pawn sacrifice, exactly as in the
main line. } )
( 14. Qc2 { An attempt to reach c7 at once. } 14... g4 { Kotronias's recommendation, which he gives two
exclamation marks: Black answers a threat to c7 by attacking. } 15. Nb5 g3 { [%cal Gd8h4,Gc8h3] The pawn
on g3 changes everything. If White closes the kingside with 16.h3 his king is locked in and Black
prepares ...Bxh3 at leisure. Against the critical 16.Bb4 and 16.Ba5 Kotronias gives 16...Nfxd5, clearing
the diagonal from d8 to h4 at the cost of a piece, with at least a draw for Black in every line. His
summary is worth remembering: once a pawn stands on g3, the d8-h4 diagonal is what matters. } )
14... Ng6 15. cxd6
( 15. Nb5 { White keeps the tension on d6. } 15... Rf7 16. Ba5 { Provoking a weakness. } 16... b6 17. cxd6
cxd6 18. Be1 g4 { Black has been made to play ...b6, which weakens c6, and White's bishop has taken three
moves to reach e1. Black goes straight for the king. } )
15... cxd6 16. Nb5 Rf7 { [%csl Yc7] } 17. Qc2 { [%cal Rb5c7] } 17... Ne8 { The one purely defensive
move. Queen and knight both aim at c7, and the rook alone is not enough. The knight will return. } 18. a4
h5 19. Nf2 { White covers g4 and h3 before Black gets there. } 19... Bf8 { [%cal Gf7g7] Covers d6 and
makes room for the rook. The a7-pawn is left hanging on purpose. }
20. h3
( 20. Nxa7 { The pawn can be taken, and this is the tactical point of Black's set-up. } 20... Rc7 { The
rook hits the queen and the knight on a7 has no way back. } 21. Ba5 Rxc2 22. Bxd8 Rxe2 23. Nxc8 Rxa4
{ Material is level and Black's rooks are active. Analysts who distrust this ending play 19...Bd7 first,
with the bishop then tied to the queenside; the main line is the principled choice. } )
20... Rg7 21. Qb3 Nh4 { [%csl Rg2,Rf3] The knight takes up its post. } 22. Rc2 a6 { Now that everything is
ready on the kingside the knight is sent away; the hole on b6 no longer matters. } 23. Na3 Nf6 { The
knight returns for the breakthrough. } 24. Be1 g4 { The break comes as a pawn sacrifice, because White
has h3 and f3 covered three times. } 25. hxg4 hxg4 26. Nxg4 Nh5 { [%cal Gh5g3,Gd8g5,Gf8e7] **Verdict:** the
theory ends here. For the pawn Black has g3 for the knight, an open h-file, the queen coming to g5 or h4
and the rook on g7 behind it all, while White's queenside has no target left. The line has been tested for
decades and Black's compensation is regarded as full: it is White who has to find the only moves. } *

[Line "10.Nd3 f5 11.f3 f4 12.b4"]
[Section "Mar del Plata: 9.Ne1"]
[Summary "White leaves the bishop on c1 and pushes the queenside pawns as far as they will go. Black ignores them and plays ...h5 and ...g4."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. f3
{ White can also start with this move; 11...f4 12.Bd2 g5 13.c5 Nf6 then transposes to the 11.Bd2 lines. }
11... f4 12. b4 { [%cal Rc4c5,Ra2a4] The bishop stays at home, where it is not in the way, and every
tempo goes into the pawn storm: c5, a4-a5 and a6 or c6. } 12... g5 13. c5 Nf6 14. a4 h5 { With no bishop
on d2 or f2 White's kingside has fewer defenders, so Black can go for ...g4 without the full regrouping. }
15. a5 Ng6 16. Nf2 { The knight is needed against ...g4. } 16... Rf7 { [%cal Gg7f8,Gf7g7,Gg5g4] Still the
right square: it covers c7 and b7 along the rank against cxd6, a6 and c6 ideas.

**Verdict:** a pure race. In Thybo-Jones, 2021, Black preferred 16...Kh8 and after 17.a6 bxa6 18.c6 gave up
the queenside completely for ...g4, ...Bh6 and ...Nh4, winning a wild game. The lesson is the same either
way: White's pawns can reach the sixth rank without creating a target, and Black must not be distracted. } *

[Line "10.f3 f5 11.g4: the Benko Attack"]
[Section "Mar del Plata: 9.Ne1"]
[Summary "White takes space on the kingside himself, to meet ...f4 with a blockade. Black keeps the tension and regroups with ...Kh8 and ...Ng8."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. f3 { A
flexible move. After 10...f5 White can still transpose with 11.Be3 f4 12.Bf2 g5 or 11.Nd3 f4. } 10... f5
11. g4 { [%csl Yg4,Yf5] Benko's idea. White wants Black to close the kingside, after which he has a free
hand on the other wing. } 11... Kh8 { [%cal Ge7g8,Gg7h6] Black does not oblige. The king leaves the g-file
and the diagonal, and g8 is cleared for the knight, which will come to f6 while the bishop goes to h6 to
exchange itself for White's good bishop. }
( 11... f4?! { What White is hoping for. } 12. h4 { The kingside is closed: ...g5 is met by h5 and ...h5 by
g5, and Black has no pawn break left while White plays c5 undisturbed. } )
12. Nd3
( 12. Be3 { The bishop avoids the exchange on h6 for the moment. } 12... Ng8 { The same plan: ...Bh6 next,
or ...f4 only now, when the bishop has to lose a move. } )
12... Ng8 { [%cal Gg7h6,Gg8f6,Gd7c5] **Verdict:** Black keeps the tension on f5 and g4 and improves his
pieces: ...Bh6, ...Ngf6, ...a5 and ...Nc5. White's king is the more exposed if the position opens, and
his extra space on the kingside is something he must defend for the rest of the game. Chances are equal. } *
